 - `sys_user_path()` resolves to the app's Documents directory on iOS. CoopDX's normal filesystem/mod code uses this write root; mods and DynOS packs remain under the existing `mods` and `dynos` layouts and are rediscovered by the normal mod/DynOS managers.
 - `platform_ios.m` has a `UIDocumentPicker` path for importing a ROM. The Mods panel exposes the app's Mods folder in Files, and `gfx_sdl.c` routes SDL `DROPFILE` events through `mod_import_file()`. This branch adds an **Import Mod ZIP** action using `UIDocumentPicker`; the selected file is copied to a unique temporary path and queued through SDL so existing import and DynOS refresh logic runs on the game event thread.
 - The production ZIP importer detects Lua/DynOS content, then extracts into existing Mods or DynOS directories; normal refresh/load code handles activation and persistence. It is not a new package/runtime format. ZIP preflight rejects unsafe paths, more than 16,384 entries, entries above 128 MiB, or total uncompressed contents above 512 MiB. Extraction streams to disk instead of allocating whole files. The scan validates every archive member even after it finds a Lua script.
-- Added host path-validation tests with GCC and Clang ASan/UBSan. A production importer end-to-end ZIP fixture test and transactional rollback remain outstanding; extraction can leave partial files if a later member fails. UIKit and the complete import workflow are not Apple-compiled or device-tested because this environment has no Xcode.
+- Added a preflight module called by the production importer and host fixtures built using the bundled miniz ZIP writer. It verifies Lua/DynOS detection, nested script folder extraction metadata, mixed-asset classification, and rejection of a traversal entry after the first Lua file. This does not yet exercise actual installation/extraction, mod startup, or rollback; extraction can leave partial files if a later member fails. UIKit and the complete import workflow are not Apple-compiled or device-tested because this environment has no Xcode.
 - Bundled example fixtures cover useful acceptance cases: `low-gravity.lua`, behavior replacement/addition and spawned objects, nested custom-animation examples for Lua and DynOS, custom HUD textures, texture overrides, custom levels, and a custom actor/collision model. These are candidate end-to-end checks; no runtime mod test has yet been claimed.
 - Import hardening is a prerequisite for treating arbitrary downloaded ZIPs as safe. Extraction still writes directly into installed folders and can leave partial files if a later entry fails; add an importer-level fixture test and consider rollback before treating untrusted ZIP installation as robust.
 
